WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.534
-Our next guest is a three-time

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.701
Grammy Award-winning
musician and songwriter.

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:06.968
Her memoir, "Don't Tell Anybody
The Secrets I Told You"

00:00:06.968 --> 00:00:08.367
is on sale now.

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:10.868
Please welcome back to the show,
Lucinda Williams, everyone.

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:13.033
[ Applause ]

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:16.267
I'm so happy to have you back.
-Thank you.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.200
-It must be a trip
to be here in New York City.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.467
You used to play outside
on 54th and 7th,

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.234
just a few blocks away from here

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:27.234
for basically, loose change,
-Right. For tips? Yeah.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:28.534
In the '70s.

00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:31.434
-Were New Yorkers polite
in the '70s?

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:33.100
-Yeah, they were.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.334
-Oh, that's --
You don't hear that very often.

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:38.100
Now, this is a memoir you wrote,

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.267
and obviously you've been
a lyricist for years.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:45.067
Did you find it easier or harder
to write your stories

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:46.467
compared to writing songs?

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:47.868
-Harder.
-Really?

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:51.133
-Yeah, it's just
a different animal altogether.

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:52.667
-But these are things
that happened to you.

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.067
Isn't it easier?

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:58.234
-Yeah, but just the actual
sitting down and writing it.

00:00:58.234 --> 00:01:00.901
I mean, like,
when I write a song,

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:03.434
I'm kind of more able to,

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:07.033
you know,
wait till I'm in the mood,

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:09.334
for the inspiration
and all of that.

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:13.167
And with this, I mean,
it's a lot of work.

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:17.000
I wrote it all by hand.
-Did you really?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.734
-Do you always write by hand
with songs as well?

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:21.300
-I don't know how to type.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.200
It's one of my regrets in life,
that I never learned to type.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.467
-Yeah, I mean,
I get for a song lyric,

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:30.033
like, there's something very
romantic about writing it out,

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:31.667
but I imagine for a memoir,
at some point,

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:34.100
you really did wish
you'd taken that typing class.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.434
-Yes.

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:39.100
-You talk about, your father
was a literary professor

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.567
and very strict on grammar.
-Yes.

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:45.534
-Do you still sort of
hear his echo

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:48.267
when you're writing,
as far as grammar?

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:53.200
-Yes, and there were many times
I was wishing he was with me

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.300
to help me, you know, just be
able to answer questions.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.434
-Was he at least humorous
about grammar or...?

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:03.000
-Oh, yeah.
He was real humorous.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.834
He had this joke he used
to tell all the time about,

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:12.834
one guy asked the other one,

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:15.367
"Where's the restaurant at?"

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:17.968
And then he would say,

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:19.534
"Don't you know
you're not supposed

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:22.367
to follow a question
with a preposition?"

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:24.601
And so then the other guy says,

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:26.868
"Okay, where's the
restaurant at, [bleep]?"

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:27.934
-There you go.

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:30.667
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:33.133
[ Applause ]

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:36.133
-Now, I guess this makes sense.

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:38.067
You've always been
a bit of a rebel.

00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:41.400
You write about,
you got suspended from school

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.000
for refusing to say
the Pledge of Allegiance.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.767
-Yeah, when we were
in Vietnam, I was protesting.

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:50.968
That was my way of protesting.

00:02:50.968 --> 00:02:53.133
-Did you have other classmates

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:55.200
who were willing to go
with you on this protest?

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.734
-Yeah, there were a couple.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.234
-And then you got suspended,

00:02:59.234 --> 00:03:01.000
but your dad stood
right up for you.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.767
-Yeah, he was a rebel, too.
-That's good.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:07.901
So what did he do to
to stop the suspension?

00:03:07.901 --> 00:03:10.634
-Well,

00:03:10.634 --> 00:03:14.000
so I went home
from school that day

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.267
and I told him what happened,

00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:17.267
and he said,
"That's okay, honey.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:21.968
We'll get an ACLU lawyer
and get you back into school."

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:24.601
And he did that,
and I got back in.

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:25.701
-Oh, that's fantastic.

00:03:25.701 --> 00:03:29.300
He was thinking ahead.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.501
Now, I imagine you make songs
and they go out in the world,

00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:37.100
and it must be special
when you hear them places

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.634
you're not expecting
to hear them.

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:39.734
Maybe you hear it on the radio,

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:41.234
maybe you hear it
in a restaurant.

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:45.100
But you heard
that one of your songs --

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.133
you were told had been used
in a pornographic film?

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:50.367
-Yes.

00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:52.601
-And they did not get
your permission for this?

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:54.868
-No, I signed it --

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:59.100
The publishing deal I signed
was not good, you know?

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.667
So I guess there was a loophole
in the contract or something,

00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:07.734
and they were able to do this
without asking me first.

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.067
-Did you at least feel

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:13.133
that they had used the spirit
of the song correctly?

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:16.067
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:17.868
-That's a loaded question.

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:19.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.968
-Well, now, what was the title?

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:22.133
What was the title of the song?

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:24.067
-They probably did use
the song correctly,

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:27.167
because it was called
"Just Another One Night Stand."

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.267
-Alright. Well...

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:30.868
Yeah, they tend not --
those films

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:32.567
don't tend to have
the longer relationships.

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:36.567
-It involved

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:40.234
a young woman wearing
a cowboy hat and cowboy boots,

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:47.934
a gas station and a couple
of cowboys and a pickup truck.

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:49.701
That's all we're going to say.

00:04:49.701 --> 00:04:51.467
We don't need to keep
talking about it.

00:04:51.467 --> 00:04:53.701
-If you've seen
one of them ever,

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:56.100
you can probably finish that.
-Pretty much, yes.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.067
Well, I'll tell you
the name of it.

00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.467
-The name of the movie?
-Yes.

00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:02.133
-Okay, it was called --

00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:04.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:08.033
You're making me laugh.

00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:11.367
It was called
"All-American Girls In Heat."

00:05:11.367 --> 00:05:12.801
-"Part Two."
-Part two?

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:15.200
Part two, yeah.
-Well, that's good.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.501
-I mean, and do you think
for people

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:20.667
who are looking it up,
they should see the first one?

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.767
Do you think they'll be
able to follow

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:24.767
the plot of the second one
if they haven't?

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:25.934
-Yeah, I think they'll be able

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:29.901
to pick up right
where the other one left off.

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:33.567
-Now, you have a new album
coming out as well this summer.

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:35.400
-Yes.
-That's very exciting.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.000
Collaborated with
some people on it.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.868
Your husband, a producer?
-Yes, I did.

00:05:39.868 --> 00:05:43.234
-And Bruce Springsteen provided
some backup vocals for you.

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:48.000
-Yes, he and his wife Patti
both sang on some of the songs.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.000
-Wonderful people.
-Yes, wonderful people.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.300
-Do you enjoy a collaboration?

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:53.601
Is that something
you've always enjoyed?

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:55.100
Have you come to enjoy it more

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.434
the longer you've been
doing this?

00:05:56.434 --> 00:06:02.234
-Well, I haven't always
collaborated in songwriting.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:06.701
But then when I started,
I tried it out.

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:09.000
It's got to be the right people.
-Yeah.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.133
-So I've actually been enjoying

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:16.100
working with my husband
on songs and also

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.267
another friend of ours,
who's also my tour manager

00:06:20.267 --> 00:06:23.567
and a musician, Travis Stephens.

00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:28.334
He worked alongside Tom and me.

00:06:28.334 --> 00:06:32.934
And then another friend of ours,
Jesse Malin,

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:37.534
who's a rock 'n roll guy
here in New York City,

00:06:37.534 --> 00:06:42.133
and he brought a good
rock 'n roll element

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:43.534
into the collaboration.

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:45.334
-I'm very excited to hear it.

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:48.667
Now, you mentioned your husband,
and obviously, he's in music.

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.567
You're in music,
and you did a very special thing

00:06:50.567 --> 00:06:52.701
for your friends
and also some unknowing fans.

00:06:52.701 --> 00:06:57.167
You guys got married
at one of your concerts.

00:06:57.167 --> 00:06:58.567
So your friends knew,

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:01.934
but the audience got to see
a Lucinda Williams concert,

00:07:01.934 --> 00:07:04.067
and then they got to watch you
get married on stage.

00:07:04.067 --> 00:07:06.400
-Yes.
-And so this is, here you are.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.934
This is Minnesota?
-Yes.

00:07:08.934 --> 00:07:10.534
[ Applause ]

00:07:10.534 --> 00:07:12.167
-And I would imagine
that was a special night,

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:14.267
not just for you
and your friends and loved ones,

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.300
but also for the people
that were there.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.767
-Yeah.
-Because you have fans -

00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:19.501
I feel this way about you.

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:21.133
I feel like you write
in a personal way.

00:07:21.133 --> 00:07:22.467
I feel like I know you.

00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:23.834
Your fans feel like
they know you,

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:25.400
and they've often
sent you things

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.167
that you might not expect
a fan would send.

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:29.200
-Yes.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.968
-What are some
of the weirder ones?

00:07:31.968 --> 00:07:36.834
-Well, one fan
sent some lingerie

00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:38.767
to my manager's address.

00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:43.934
And that was unusual because,

00:07:43.934 --> 00:07:47.200
I mean, you know, how would
they know what size to get?

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.400
-Yeah, exactly.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.868
-Did someone send you?
Is it accurate,

00:07:52.868 --> 00:07:54.767
someone sent you
a bag of onions?

00:07:54.767 --> 00:07:56.033
-Yes.

00:07:56.033 --> 00:08:00.234
-A bag of Vidalia onions,
not just any onion.

00:08:00.234 --> 00:08:03.801
Vidalia onions are,
they're very good.

00:08:03.801 --> 00:08:08.467
They have sort
of a sweet taste a little bit.

00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:09.968
-Well, I think it says
a lot about you

00:08:09.968 --> 00:08:12.834
that given a choice between
lingerie and Vidalia onions,

00:08:12.834 --> 00:08:14.400
you're like, onions every time.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.200
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.701
Well, what a delight
to have you here.

00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:21.200
What a delight
to read your memoir.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.567
I'm very excited that you have
new music coming out.

00:08:22.567 --> 00:08:23.968
-Thank you.

00:08:23.968 --> 00:08:26.400
-It is just such a lovely thing
to spend time with you.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:27.501
-Thank you, Seth.

00:08:27.501 --> 00:08:30.734
-You guys,
that's Lucinda Williams.

00:08:30.734 --> 00:08:32.467
-"Don't Tell Anybody
The Secrets I Told You"

00:08:32.467 --> 00:08:33.701
is on sale now.

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:35.467
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

